D2365 power-off and normal-restart regression checks
=================================================

Run from this source checkout:

    make -C tools/testing/selftests/d2365 run_tests

Python 3 and an existing host C compiler (cc or clang) are required. The
suite compiles the actual driver algorithms against an in-memory regmap
and power-supply shim. It does not execute firmware, access devices, call
a reboot syscall, build a kernel, or prove electrical power-off. This is
a source-tree test: an installed selftests bundle without the matching
kernel sources is not sufficient.

Coverage
--------

* All 256 GPIO configuration byte values, including the <= 0x3f boundary.
  Between the initial and final 36-byte reads, the mock changes both even
  and odd bytes: writes must retain transformed initial configurations and
  freshly read odd paired bytes. The second read immediately precedes the
  bulk write. The six extra GPIO registers retain their snapshot behavior.
* Exact shutdown order, event reads rather than event writes, event masks,
  RTC value, boot state preservation for all 256 values, and a forced
  standby write even when its request bit is already set.
* Every nonzero source-status byte, nonzero supply values, unknown source
  state, and source attachment during each live recheck window.
* Every transaction error on five source routes, with failed writes that
  commit nothing, commit before returning an error, or partially commit a
  bulk transfer. The driver stops without retry, reports unknown outcomes
  for attempted standby transactions, and never records an acknowledged
  standby on an error. The mock separately counts attempted and committed
  bus writes; a regmap transaction attempt includes its internal read.
* Ordinary reboot has exactly two transactions: read 30 bytes at 0x0140,
  then write zero to 0x5001. Either failure is logged, and event-read
  failure does not suppress the boot-stage write. No power-off-only writes
  or delays occur. This callback does not perform the watchdog reset.
* Final power-off performs no bus access or sleep. The restart shim exits
  via longjmp because arm64 machine_restart never returns, even when reset
  fails and the architecture halts. Reaching the final callback after an
  acknowledged standby reports failure, not success.
* Prepare/final handler registration and the actual GPIO shutdown drain
  callback, with lock and gate-order assertions. The source assertions are
  not a substitute for exercising kernel notifier/device lifecycle paths.

Evidence and intentional Linux differences
-----------------------------------------

The sequence is an independent Linux implementation informed by J71b
D2365 firmware, not Apple's binary driver and not identical Apple behavior.
The analyzed kernelcache SHA-256 is:
5c0822a78a10191b56edd468f100b331b0e86bcafa3fdcc5d974dadc427dbec9
No firmware bytes or disassembly are included.

The verified GPIO helper refreshes the 36-byte block, overlays the saved
configuration bytes at even offsets, writes the block, then writes six
extra registers. Ordinary Apple restart separately acknowledges events and
clears boot stage before watchdog reset; its failure path still resets.

Linux intentionally rejects unknown source state, rechecks live sources,
stops the shutdown sequence on transaction errors, keeps a bounded 1000 ms
grace period, and uses sleepable sys-off preparation. The 0x90 boot-state
bits belong to the source-status branch, not to ordinary reboot. Scheduled
RTC/UPO wake, separate external shutdown controllers, and Apple's panic
or PHRA flows are not implemented here. The existing watchdog is unchanged.

Validation still required
-------------------------

No full kernel build, olddefconfig resolution, DT schema check, or device
boot/power test has been performed for this branch. Both Orchard defconfigs
carry the new driver enable, but only J71b instantiates the power-off child.
The shared GPIO shutdown change also affects other instantiated D2365 PMU
event domains; regression testing on other such boards remains necessary.
An exact-revision J71b hardware run is required before exporting Orchard
patches or calling the power-off feature validated.
